  "summary": "Give and understand advice with لَازِم أَنْ… and يَجِبُ أَنْ… (must, have to), and say that something is not necessary.",
  "intro": "On Thursday Daniel goes back to Dr Fatima's clinic. He feels much better and wants to get back to school and to football, but the doctor has advice. To say 'must', use لَازِم (necessary) or the more formal يَجِبُ, then أَنْ and a present-tense verb ending in -a: لَازِم أَنْ تَرْتَاحَ (you must rest), يَجِبُ أَنْ أَنَامَ (I must sleep). The words لَازِم and يَجِبُ never change; the person is on the second verb.",
  "objectives": [
    "Say what someone must do with لَازِم أَنْ and يَجِبُ أَنْ",
    "Ask 'do I have to…?' with هَلْ يَجِبُ أَنْ…؟",
    "Say 'it is not necessary' with لَيْسَ ضَرُورِيًّا",
    "Give simple health advice about rest, sleep and water"
  ],
